INT. NIGHTBIRD STUDIOS - CONTROL ROOM - NIGHT

Rain on the skylight. Amber light from the console. A dozen VU meters twitch in the dark.

Through the glass, the live room: a vocal booth, a lone mic on a stand, a grand piano under a sheet.

REGGIE, 50s, owner, silver ponytail, a cardigan with a hole in the elbow, sits at the board. Beside him, SHAY, 20s, the artist, hoodie up, headphones around her neck. By the door, leaning on the wall with a paper cup of coffee gone cold, OMAR, 40s, in a wool coat that is too good for the room. And on the leather couch, KAT, 30s, her laptop open on her knees, typing.

A clock above the door. 1:12.

Thunder, distant.

ON SCREEN: Kat's laptop. An email, subject line "Master Delivery - FINAL", sent 11:58 PM, from an address the label has never used.

REGGIE
Run it back.

He hits a key. The speakers fill with a vocal take, ghostly, a voice of ice. It ends. Silence.

SHAY
That's the one.

OMAR
That's not the one.

SHAY
Omar.

OMAR
That's a take. That's a very nice take. It's not the one that goes on the record.

SHAY
It's the one I meant.

OMAR
You meant it in July. You meant it in July with a different man in the room.

Reggie shifts in his chair. The meters twitch.

SHAY
Don't.

OMAR
I'm just saying where the money is.

The thunder again, closer.

KAT
(without looking up)
The wire cleared at eleven fifty-eight. Both accounts. Just so everybody knows.

Reggie stops. Turns slowly.

REGGIE
What wire?

KAT
Oh. Nothing. Forget I said it.

SHAY
Kat.

OMAR ^
Kat.

REGGIE
(standing)
What wire, Kat?

She closes the laptop. Everything in the room seems to lean toward her. Rain drums harder on the skylight.

KAT
Reggie, you've been mixing other people's lies for thirty years. You really want to hear this one in your own studio?

REGGIE
In my studio, yes.

KAT
The album isn't hers. Never was. The masters belong to a company in Nevada that belongs to a company in Delaware that belongs to Omar. Shay signed it in July. She didn't read it. Reggie, you didn't read it. I read it. I'm the only one who ever read it.

REGGIE
You work for me.

KAT
I work for whoever pays the rent on the building you're about to lose.

SHAY
(very quietly)
Omar. Is that true?

OMAR
Shay, it's a standard deal.

SHAY
Is it true?

OMAR
It's business. It was always going to be business. You wanted to be heard by two million people, somebody has to own the room.

SHAY
You sat with me for six months. You told me my father's songs mattered.

OMAR
They do. They're worth a lot.

A long stillness. Shay takes the headphones from around her neck and sets them very gently on the console. Reggie sits back down heavily. He looks suddenly old.

REGGIE
Thirty years. Never once did I let anyone take the tape out of this building.

KAT
The tape is the least of it.

Rain. Thunder breaks directly overhead. In the live room, the piano sheet slides slowly off its shoulders and falls to the floor. The lights flicker. When they come back, Omar is standing in front of the door, one hand on the knob, and he is no longer smiling.

OMAR
Nobody's walking out of here with a copy.

Shay reaches for the red button on the console marked RECORD, and presses it.
